{"id":"bef8326e-beab-4740-b40d-db6fe7cbafa5","recipient":"a228afbf-3d9a-4a60-8ce6-ace5f878390a","sender":"d176c90f-1f79-4419-85a7-14ac079b3d33","priority":"MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH","timestamp":"2026-09-14T09:56:58.494522500Z","renderDetails":{"messageTitle":"Message from Formatter (research)"},"content":"---**I TESTS FOR LIGAMENT STABILITY** ---**1 ANTERIOR DRAWER TEST ANKLE** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the anterior talofibular ligament ATFL and lateral ankle stability PROCEDURE • Patient sits or lies supine • Keep the ankle in a neutral or slight plantar flexed position • Stabilize the distal tibia with one hand • Grasp the heel/talus with the other hand • Draw the foot/talus anteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive anterior translation compared with the opposite ankle • Soft/absent end feel • Pain may be reproduced INDICATES ATFL injury/lateral ankle instability ---**2 TALAR TILT TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the lateral ankle ligaments particularly the calcaneofibular ligament CFL PROCEDURE • Patient sits or lies supine • Stabilize the distal leg • Grasp the calcaneus/heel • Tilt the calcaneus into inversion POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive inversion compared with the opposite side • Pain or a soft end feel INDICATES CFL injury/lateral ankle instability ---**II TESTS FOR VASCULAR CONDITIONS DVT** ---**3 HOMANS TEST** PURPOSE Traditionally used to assess for deep vein thrombosis DVT of the lower limb PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee is extended • Examiner supports the leg • Passively dorsiflex the ankle POSITIVE SIGN • Pain in the calf during passive ankle dorsiflexion INDICATES Traditionally considered suggestive of DVT ---**4 MOSES TEST** PURPOSE Traditionally used to assess for DVT PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with the leg relaxed • Compress the calf anteroposteriorly • Then compress the calf laterally POSITIVE SIGN • Pain produced by anteroposterior compression but not by lateral compression INDICATES Traditionally considered suggestive of DVT"